  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "The sum of two positive numbers is \\(5\\) times their difference. What is the ratio of the larger number to the smaller number?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{5}\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Their difference must be positive, because their sum is positive and equals five times that difference. Take the difference as one unit. Then their sum is five units, and the larger number has one unit more than the smaller.\n\nRemoving that extra unit from the sum leaves four units consisting of two equal copies of the smaller number. Thus the smaller number is two units and the larger is three units. Their ratio is therefore\n\\[\n3:2=\\frac32,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
